1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,631
Businesses buy on account, quote a purchase order number, and want a manager

2
00:00:05,631 --> 00:00:07,607
to approve the bigger orders.

3
00:00:07,607 --> 00:00:11,616
This video shows how a company buys from your store, how an admin

4
00:00:11,616 --> 00:00:14,440
approves an order, and how the company pays.

5
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:23,325
A business buyer spends the company's money, so the company wants a say

6
00:00:23,325 --> 00:00:24,660
in what is bought.

7
00:00:25,410 --> 00:00:30,980
It wants the order tied to its purchase order number, so accounting can match the invoice.

8
00:00:31,730 --> 00:00:33,791
And it wants to pay later:

9
00:00:33,791 --> 00:00:37,270
a small deposit now, and the rest on an invoice.

10
00:00:38,020 --> 00:00:43,250
Companies do all of that, and the buyer shops and checks out as usual.

11
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:52,180
A company has buyers and admins, who sign in as themselves.

12
00:00:52,930 --> 00:00:55,177
Its terms say how it pays:

13
00:00:55,177 --> 00:00:59,670
net days, a deposit, a credit limit, and a purchase order.

14
00:01:00,420 --> 00:01:06,110
An order limit, or a monthly budget, sends bigger orders to an admin to approve.

15
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:11,920
Once approved, the buyer pays the deposit, and the rest goes on account.

16
00:01:16,124 --> 00:01:19,686
Open Commerce, Companies, and open the company.

17
00:01:19,686 --> 00:01:24,594
Northwind Traders already has an admin, Riley, and a buyer, Morgan.

18
00:01:25,047 --> 00:01:27,036
Give a buyer an Order limit.

19
00:01:27,036 --> 00:01:31,657
Morgan may place orders up to three hundred dollars; a bigger order waits

20
00:01:31,657 --> 00:01:33,997
for a company admin to approve it.

21
00:01:34,451 --> 00:01:39,490
Under Payment terms, Net days is how long the company has to pay its invoice.

22
00:01:39,490 --> 00:01:43,376
Deposit is the part paid at checkout, here twenty percent.

23
00:01:43,376 --> 00:01:46,958
Credit limit is the most the company may owe at once:

24
00:01:46,958 --> 00:01:51,026
an order goes on account only as far as the limit allows, and

25
00:01:51,026 --> 00:01:53,151
the rest is paid at checkout.

26
00:01:53,605 --> 00:01:58,475
Monthly budget sends any order that would take the month past it to an admin too.

27
00:01:58,475 --> 00:02:02,505
And Buyers give a purchase order number makes the number required.

28
00:02:02,956 --> 00:02:04,157
Click Save.

29
00:02:04,157 --> 00:02:08,116
The company is saved, and its row shows its terms.

30
00:02:12,407 --> 00:02:16,746
Now Morgan, the buyer, signs in, and adds a watch to the cart.

31
00:02:16,746 --> 00:02:23,190
Morgan changes the quantity to three, so the order comes to three hundred eighty-seven dollars.

32
00:02:23,190 --> 00:02:26,827
That is over Morgan's order limit of three hundred.

33
00:02:27,298 --> 00:02:31,786
Checkout starts as usual, with the Contact step and the Shipping step.

34
00:02:31,786 --> 00:02:36,569
Morgan fills in the contact details, then the address where the order goes,

35
00:02:36,569 --> 00:02:38,576
and chooses a shipping rate.

36
00:02:38,576 --> 00:02:41,529
A company buyer does nothing different here:

37
00:02:41,529 --> 00:02:45,898
the extra step comes after shipping, and it is the one that matters.

38
00:02:46,349 --> 00:02:48,186
Then comes a Company step.

39
00:02:48,186 --> 00:02:52,780
It says Morgan is ordering for Northwind Traders, and that a company admin

40
00:02:52,780 --> 00:02:57,029
has to approve this order first, because it is over the order limit.

41
00:02:57,029 --> 00:03:01,279
The company requires a purchase order number, so Morgan types it in.

42
00:03:01,733 --> 00:03:04,879
Morgan ticks Ask for approval, and continues.

43
00:03:04,879 --> 00:03:10,923
The company's admins are emailed, and the page says the order is waiting for their approval.

44
00:03:11,376 --> 00:03:16,299
Under Morgan's orders, the order says it is waiting for approval, and why.

45
00:03:16,299 --> 00:03:19,436
It cannot be paid until an admin approves it.

46
00:03:23,644 --> 00:03:25,626
Riley is a company admin.

47
00:03:25,626 --> 00:03:31,127
Riley's Company page, under Your account, lists the orders waiting for approval:

48
00:03:31,127 --> 00:03:34,134
who asked, why, and what is in the order.

49
00:03:34,591 --> 00:03:37,759
Riley can add a note for the buyer, such as why it was

50
00:03:37,759 --> 00:03:39,818
approved, and then click Approve.

51
00:03:39,818 --> 00:03:44,253
Reject would cancel the order instead, so the buyer would have to start again.

52
00:03:44,253 --> 00:03:47,051
The buyer is emailed either way, with the note.

53
00:03:47,501 --> 00:03:51,793
The page also shows the company's monthly budget and what has been spent,

54
00:03:51,793 --> 00:03:55,541
and every order of the company, with its purchase order number.

55
00:03:59,768 --> 00:04:02,834
Morgan is emailed that the order is approved.

56
00:04:02,834 --> 00:04:06,802
Under Morgan's orders, the order now says Riley approved it:

57
00:04:06,802 --> 00:04:08,305
pay it to place it.

58
00:04:08,305 --> 00:04:09,988
Morgan clicks Pay now.

59
00:04:10,443 --> 00:04:13,211
Checkout opens at the Company step again.

60
00:04:13,211 --> 00:04:17,983
Morgan enters the purchase order number, and continues to the payment step.

61
00:04:18,435 --> 00:04:21,772
Beside the payment methods is a Pay on account box.

62
00:04:21,772 --> 00:04:26,162
It says Morgan pays a twenty percent deposit now, and the rest, three

63
00:04:26,162 --> 00:04:31,256
hundred sixteen dollars, goes on Northwind Traders's account, due in thirty days.

64
00:04:31,256 --> 00:04:35,646
Morgan clicks Pay on account, and the order shows what is on account,

65
00:04:35,646 --> 00:04:39,335
and that only the seventy-nine dollar deposit is due now.

66
00:04:39,789 --> 00:04:46,091
Morgan picks a payment method for the deposit, here a bank transfer, whose instructions appear below.

67
00:04:46,091 --> 00:04:50,507
Then Morgan clicks Pay now, and the order is placed, with the deposit

68
00:04:50,507 --> 00:04:52,509
due and the rest on account.

69
00:04:52,961 --> 00:04:57,223
The company's page now shows what Northwind owes on account.

70
00:04:57,223 --> 00:05:02,971
Under Balances and invoices, Morgan finds the invoice, which is due in thirty days.

71
00:05:07,161 --> 00:05:10,024
Staff see it under Commerce, Transactions.

72
00:05:10,024 --> 00:05:12,291
The order made two transactions:

73
00:05:12,291 --> 00:05:16,825
the twenty percent deposit, to be paid by bank transfer, and the rest,

74
00:05:16,825 --> 00:05:22,492
from the source company-terms, put on Northwind Traders's account with its purchase order

75
00:05:22,492 --> 00:05:24,461
number, due in thirty days.

76
00:05:24,913 --> 00:05:26,795
Open the one on account.

77
00:05:26,795 --> 00:05:31,687
Its timeline says it was put on Northwind's account, due in thirty days.

78
00:05:31,687 --> 00:05:37,583
When the company pays, enter the amount and a reference under Record an offline payment.

79
00:05:38,034 --> 00:05:41,752
Click Record payment, and confirm that it counts as received.

80
00:05:41,752 --> 00:05:46,135
The transaction shows what is paid, and what is still outstanding, so you

81
00:05:46,135 --> 00:05:48,354
always know what the company owes.

82
00:05:52,542 --> 00:05:55,777
A buyer who wants a price first can ask for a quote.

83
00:05:55,777 --> 00:06:00,909
When quote requests are turned on, under Settings, Commerce, Orders, a signed-in buyer

84
00:06:00,909 --> 00:06:03,084
sees Request a quote on the cart.

85
00:06:03,084 --> 00:06:05,482
Morgan wants twenty of these watches.

86
00:06:05,933 --> 00:06:10,025
Morgan writes a message, such as how many are needed and by when,

87
00:06:10,025 --> 00:06:12,445
and gives the purchase order number.

88
00:06:12,445 --> 00:06:16,536
The cart is emptied, and the request shows under Morgan's orders.

89
00:06:16,536 --> 00:06:21,433
Staff then price it and send the quote, and the company pays it like any order.

90
00:06:25,633 --> 00:06:30,756
A company's procurement system, such as SAP, can open your store by punchout,

91
00:06:30,756 --> 00:06:34,273
and the buyer fills a cart at the company's prices.

92
00:06:35,023 --> 00:06:40,772
On the cart, Transfer to your procurement system sends the items, quantities, prices

93
00:06:40,772 --> 00:06:44,221
and SKUs back, to be approved and ordered there.

94
00:06:44,221 --> 00:06:49,713
You turn punchout on in the company, as the Customers and Companies video shows.

95
00:06:53,913 --> 00:06:55,495
A trade supplier:

96
00:06:55,495 --> 00:07:00,653
a purchase order, a small deposit, and an invoice due in thirty days.

97
00:07:01,403 --> 00:07:03,268
A multi-site business:

98
00:07:03,268 --> 00:07:07,663
buyers order up to a limit, and a manager approves the rest.

99
00:07:08,413 --> 00:07:09,850
A monthly budget:

100
00:07:09,850 --> 00:07:12,973
orders that would pass it wait for an admin.

101
00:07:13,723 --> 00:07:14,964
A large order:

102
00:07:14,964 --> 00:07:18,933
a quote with a purchase order, paid at the price you send.

103
00:07:20,933 --> 00:07:25,317
With companies, your business customers buy the way their business works:

104
00:07:25,317 --> 00:07:28,591
on account, with approvals, against a purchase order.

105
00:07:28,591 --> 00:07:30,033
Thanks for watching.

